<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android platform boundary manifest.

  Still deliberately empty of permissions, components and intent filters - now by
  a measured finding rather than by absence of code. Phase 1 left it empty because
  nothing was implemented (ADR-P0-001: a declaration with no call behind it is a
  fabricated capability). Phase 2 then verified that an app targeting API 31 or
  above needs no permission to obtain the adapter, read its state, register for
  ACTION_STATE_CHANGED, inspect permission standing or query Bluetooth feature
  flags, so the module gained its mechanism and no uses-permission (ADR-P2-011,
  bluetooth-api-research.md Q1/Q7).

  A library manifest merges into every consumer APK silently, which makes
  over-declaration expensive in exactly the way a false capability claim is.
  Permissions are added by the phase that actually performs the operation they
  gate, with a justification in that phase's security section. This emptiness is
  machine-checked by platformManifestDeclaresNothingUnjustified, which also refuses a
  receiver, service, activity or provider element.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android" />
